WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.231 --> 00:00:01.527
(loud whoosh)

00:00:01.527 --> 00:00:03.037
(bang)

00:00:03.037 --> 00:00:04.213
- What's up, Believe Nation?

00:00:04.213 --> 00:00:06.462
So in January, I got really sick

00:00:06.462 --> 00:00:09.447
and made it a goal for myself
to never get sick again,

00:00:09.447 --> 00:00:11.957
to always be healthy,
and a key part of that

00:00:11.957 --> 00:00:14.526
was going to be studying
the methods of Wim Hof,

00:00:14.526 --> 00:00:17.267
applying it to my life,
and seeing if it worked.

00:00:17.267 --> 00:00:18.410
We made our first video.

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:19.725
It did really well.

00:00:19.725 --> 00:00:20.723
A lot of you guys were asking,

00:00:20.723 --> 00:00:23.512
feedback in the comments
afterwards, "Does this really work?

00:00:23.512 --> 00:00:24.687
"What was the impact?"

00:00:24.687 --> 00:00:26.200
And so today we're going to follow along

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.061
investigative journalist and
author of What Doesn't Kill Us,

00:00:29.061 --> 00:00:31.673
Scott Carney, as he investigates,

00:00:31.673 --> 00:00:35.276
does the Wim Hof method actually work?

00:00:35.276 --> 00:00:36.984
(techno music)

00:00:36.984 --> 00:00:39.697
(inspirational music)

00:00:39.697 --> 00:00:43.507
- I traveled to Poland
to expose a charlatan,

00:00:43.507 --> 00:00:45.791
a prophet who claimed he could give

00:00:45.791 --> 00:00:48.445
ordinary people superpowers.

00:00:48.445 --> 00:00:51.521
He said he could control his
body temperature at will,

00:00:51.521 --> 00:00:53.533
hijack his immune system,

00:00:53.533 --> 00:00:55.838
and conquer the world's tallest mountains

00:00:55.838 --> 00:00:58.707
with no cold weather gear.

00:00:58.707 --> 00:01:01.539
I'd built my career as an
investigative journalist

00:01:01.539 --> 00:01:04.207
debunking people like him.

00:01:04.207 --> 00:01:07.418
But to prove he was a liar or a madman,

00:01:07.418 --> 00:01:09.418
I had to try his method.

00:01:10.287 --> 00:01:12.485
I'm not an athlete, I'm not exceptional,

00:01:12.485 --> 00:01:16.937
but I found a power that
I never knew humans had.

00:01:16.937 --> 00:01:20.992
I hiked to the summit of Mount
Kilimanjaro without a shirt.

00:01:20.992 --> 00:01:23.673
I sat on the snowy bank of a river

00:01:23.673 --> 00:01:26.267
until the ice melted around me.

00:01:26.267 --> 00:01:28.600
I trained with top athletes.

00:01:29.612 --> 00:01:31.700
I crossed the finish line of the world's

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:35.004
most notorious cold weather
obstacle course race

00:01:35.004 --> 00:01:38.696
wearing nothing but shorts and a smile.

00:01:38.696 --> 00:01:41.414
I can do 80 pushups on a single breath,

00:01:41.414 --> 00:01:45.464
and I lost seven pounds
of fat in seven days.

00:01:45.464 --> 00:01:47.714
If I can do it, anyone can.

00:01:49.437 --> 00:01:51.786
(guitar music)

00:01:51.786 --> 00:01:53.996
I'm Scott Carney, and I'm
an investigative journalist,

00:01:53.996 --> 00:01:58.163
and I'm here to climb Kilimanjaro
with Wim Hof, the madman.

00:01:59.143 --> 00:02:02.484
We're going to do it in just two
days 'till we get to the top.

00:02:02.484 --> 00:02:03.965
We're doing it because we can,

00:02:03.965 --> 00:02:06.471
because this is the way to show

00:02:06.471 --> 00:02:08.490
our bodies' physical potential

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:12.462
by unlocking some of what
evolution has given us

00:02:12.462 --> 00:02:16.472
using very simple techniques,
breathing and cold exposure

00:02:16.472 --> 00:02:17.994
and mental focus.

00:02:17.994 --> 00:02:19.510
We feel like if we have all of that,

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:21.690
we're going to do something
really, really special.

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:24.482
So the normal ascent is
between five and eight days

00:02:24.482 --> 00:02:27.231
to get to the summit of Kilimanjaro.

00:02:27.231 --> 00:02:28.790
We're doing it in two.

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:32.924
When I asked the US
Army's environmental unit

00:02:32.924 --> 00:02:34.407
what our success rate should be,

00:02:34.407 --> 00:02:36.044
they said that we would have about

00:02:36.044 --> 00:02:38.940
60 to 75 percent of the
people in this group

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:41.013
come down with acute mountain sickness.

00:02:41.013 --> 00:02:43.787
They think that most of us are
not going to make it to the top,

00:02:43.787 --> 00:02:46.789
and we're here to prove
that this technique

00:02:46.789 --> 00:02:49.622
will let most of us get to the top,

00:02:49.622 --> 00:02:52.289
hopefully all of us, and safely.

00:02:57.448 --> 00:03:01.365
(suspenseful percussion music)

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:31.927
(suspenseful percussion music)

00:03:55.143 --> 00:03:56.643
- This is awesome.

00:04:06.124 --> 00:04:07.311
- Oh!

00:04:07.311 --> 00:04:08.728
- Yeah, right up.

00:04:10.251 --> 00:04:14.007
- Holy (audio cuts out)

00:04:14.007 --> 00:04:15.420
- [Man] That is not easy, huh?

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:17.941
- It got cold in the valley.

00:04:17.941 --> 00:04:21.858
(suspenseful percussion music)

00:04:55.465 --> 00:04:58.632
(inspirational music)

00:05:11.554 --> 00:05:15.137
- We have done it with
the Wim Hof himself,

00:05:16.274 --> 00:05:17.444
with Salim.

00:05:17.444 --> 00:05:18.277
Woo-hoo!

00:05:18.277 --> 00:05:19.287
- Salim, our guide.

00:05:19.287 --> 00:05:20.245
He set the pace.

00:05:20.245 --> 00:05:21.411
He was amazing.

00:05:21.411 --> 00:05:25.214
And we got Dennis here, the man
who looks better in a onesie

00:05:25.214 --> 00:05:26.506
than I've ever seen in my life.

00:05:26.506 --> 00:05:28.236
Look at this beautiful onesie.

00:05:28.236 --> 00:05:30.444
- Wind and snow, man.

00:05:30.444 --> 00:05:32.015
Cold wind and snow.

00:05:32.015 --> 00:05:33.765
- Yeah, it's snowing.

00:05:34.691 --> 00:05:37.503
Didn't go bare chested the
whole way, but you know what?

00:05:37.503 --> 00:05:38.750
That doesn't matter.

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:39.976
We did it.

00:05:39.976 --> 00:05:43.143
(inspirational music)

00:05:58.222 --> 00:05:59.952
So I'm an investigative journalist,

00:05:59.952 --> 00:06:02.511
and over the course of my career,

00:06:02.511 --> 00:06:05.466
I have examined a lot of false gurus,

00:06:05.466 --> 00:06:09.466
people who make really
advanced spiritual claims

00:06:11.715 --> 00:06:15.856
and occasionally end with
people dying or getting hurt,

00:06:15.856 --> 00:06:17.966
losing all their money,
that sort of thing.

00:06:17.966 --> 00:06:19.641
And actually, the previous
book that I'd written,

00:06:19.641 --> 00:06:21.285
A Death on Diamond Mountain,

00:06:21.285 --> 00:06:23.501
explores not only my personal experience

00:06:23.501 --> 00:06:24.804
with a friend of mine who died

00:06:24.804 --> 00:06:27.963
on a meditation retreat in northern India,

00:06:27.963 --> 00:06:31.015
but also the death of
a man named Ian Thorson

00:06:31.015 --> 00:06:32.529
in the deserts of Arizona while studying

00:06:32.529 --> 00:06:36.459
with a very controversial
Tibetan guru named Michael Roach.

00:06:36.459 --> 00:06:38.709
And Roach made these claims

00:06:39.951 --> 00:06:43.013
that he could give you
superpowers through meditation,

00:06:43.013 --> 00:06:44.175
things like levitating,

00:06:44.175 --> 00:06:45.700
things like walking through the walls,

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:48.487
things like telepathy, these things

00:06:48.487 --> 00:06:50.453
which are in the yoga sutras

00:06:50.453 --> 00:06:52.889
considered siddhis, or miracles.

00:06:52.889 --> 00:06:55.556
Now, when I heard about Wim Hof,

00:06:56.498 --> 00:06:57.942
I thought that he was going to be

00:06:57.942 --> 00:07:01.409
another very sketchy
guru like Michael Roach,

00:07:01.409 --> 00:07:03.265
'cause his claims were that

00:07:03.265 --> 00:07:05.814
he could control his
body temperature at will,

00:07:05.814 --> 00:07:09.400
consciously control his
autonomic nervous system

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:10.977
and his immune system,

00:07:10.977 --> 00:07:13.453
which aren't quite as grandiose claims

00:07:13.453 --> 00:07:17.620
as this other guy I looked
at, but potentially dangerous,

00:07:18.664 --> 00:07:21.006
because what Wim Hof is famous for

00:07:21.006 --> 00:07:23.239
is his incredible resilience.

00:07:23.239 --> 00:07:24.719
(audio cutting out)

00:07:24.719 --> 00:07:28.260
was the picture of him
sitting on an iceberg

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:31.256
somewhere north of the
Arctic Circle in just shorts,

00:07:31.256 --> 00:07:33.441
shirtless in the cold, and
he looks sort of happy.

00:07:33.441 --> 00:07:36.858
He has the record for an Arctic marathon,

00:07:38.687 --> 00:07:41.131
again, barefoot in his shorts.

00:07:41.131 --> 00:07:43.993
He's done marathons in the Sahara.

00:07:43.993 --> 00:07:46.517
He's hiked two thirds up
the way Mount Everest,

00:07:46.517 --> 00:07:47.755
also in his shorts.

00:07:47.755 --> 00:07:50.759
And so I knew he had
some of these abilities,

00:07:50.759 --> 00:07:52.492
he'd had a history with this.

00:07:52.492 --> 00:07:54.781
However, what I worried was that

00:07:54.781 --> 00:07:57.099
when he claims that he
can teach these things,

00:07:57.099 --> 00:07:59.971
what might be his innate
biological abilities

00:07:59.971 --> 00:08:04.034
are really the false claims
that could get someone killed.

00:08:04.034 --> 00:08:07.256
So I had a assignment
from Playboy Magazine

00:08:07.256 --> 00:08:11.251
to go there and write about his training.

00:08:11.251 --> 00:08:13.520
And I got off the plane,

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:16.962
and initially, he's not
a very impressive person.

00:08:16.962 --> 00:08:18.916
He's a Dutch fitness guru.

00:08:18.916 --> 00:08:20.471
He's not incredibly tall.

00:08:20.471 --> 00:08:23.533
He's got a big ruddy nose
from years of alcoholism.

00:08:23.533 --> 00:08:25.491
He's a smoker.

00:08:25.491 --> 00:08:27.009
He doesn't smell so great.

00:08:27.009 --> 00:08:29.447
His speech is very disorganized.

00:08:29.447 --> 00:08:33.614
And I was like, "Oh, this
guy is probably a sham."

00:08:36.336 --> 00:08:37.473
So he meets me at the airport.

00:08:37.473 --> 00:08:38.306
We're in Wroclaw.

00:08:38.306 --> 00:08:40.473
This is the Polish winter.

00:08:41.778 --> 00:08:43.476
I like to say it's the same winter

00:08:43.476 --> 00:08:45.701
that stopped the Nazi army.

00:08:45.701 --> 00:08:50.238
It's the same winter that
Napoleon turned his armies around.

00:08:50.238 --> 00:08:51.444
And here I am.

00:08:51.444 --> 00:08:55.948
I'm taking a cab with him into
the mountains of that area,

00:08:55.948 --> 00:08:59.165
and he says basically
that in a couple days

00:08:59.165 --> 00:09:01.615
I'll be standing out in
my shorts and a shirt

00:09:01.615 --> 00:09:04.292
and heating myself up
consciously and have no trouble,

00:09:04.292 --> 00:09:06.962
and I thought that was sort of crazy.

00:09:06.962 --> 00:09:10.485
But, as an anthropologist, as
an investigative journalist,

00:09:10.485 --> 00:09:12.525
as somebody who believes in the facts,

00:09:12.525 --> 00:09:15.025
I can't just go there and say,

00:09:16.035 --> 00:09:19.988
just write him off full stop
without giving him a chance.

00:09:19.988 --> 00:09:22.235
So I say, "Okay, I'm going to
try your method for a few days,

00:09:22.235 --> 00:09:24.449
"and the second that I start
feeling this is sketchy,

00:09:24.449 --> 00:09:27.403
"that this is no good, I'm out."

00:09:27.403 --> 00:09:30.474
And it turns out things were pretty cool.

00:09:30.474 --> 00:09:33.721
You expose yourself to extreme-ish cold,

00:09:33.721 --> 00:09:36.044
not like space temperatures,

00:09:36.044 --> 00:09:38.212
but ice baths and things like that,

00:09:38.212 --> 00:09:41.271
and then when you're in this stimulus

00:09:41.271 --> 00:09:43.502
which would trigger your
fight or flight responses,

00:09:43.502 --> 00:09:46.752
you suppress and redirect that response

00:09:48.169 --> 00:09:49.332
to make it not happen.

00:09:49.332 --> 00:09:52.405
So to give an example, in an ice bath,

00:09:52.405 --> 00:09:54.703
you get into the ice bath
and instead of tensing up

00:09:54.703 --> 00:09:57.737
and being like, "Oh my
god, this is horrible!"

00:09:57.737 --> 00:10:00.496
you relax in that environment
and try to trigger

00:10:00.496 --> 00:10:03.240
a different way to warm your
body through metabolic action

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:06.516
instead of the tension of your muscles.

00:10:06.516 --> 00:10:10.349
And then he also has a
breathing method where,

00:10:13.538 --> 00:10:15.287
basically, you hyperventilate
and hold your breath

00:10:15.287 --> 00:10:16.521
and you hyperventilate
and hold your breath,

00:10:16.521 --> 00:10:17.726
and what you're trying to do is

00:10:17.726 --> 00:10:19.279
when you're holding your breath

00:10:19.279 --> 00:10:24.257
is stretch out that point where
you feel the need to gasp.

00:10:24.257 --> 00:10:27.507
And by doing that, you're also tweaking

00:10:28.543 --> 00:10:29.965
that fight or flight response.

00:10:29.965 --> 00:10:33.382
You're tweaking that feeling in your body

00:10:34.921 --> 00:10:37.470
that seems autonomic, but
you're actually realizing

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:40.265
that there's some negotiation
that you have over that.

00:10:40.265 --> 00:10:44.432
When you start the method,
it's this hyperventilation

00:10:45.402 --> 00:10:47.027
and then there's the cold exposure.

00:10:47.027 --> 00:10:50.223
And the first thing we did was
the hyperventilation routine.

00:10:50.223 --> 00:10:52.308
And I knew beforehand that
I could hold my breath

00:10:52.308 --> 00:10:54.431
for like 30 seconds or 45 seconds

00:10:54.431 --> 00:10:56.629
if I just like (inhales
sharply) and held it.

00:10:56.629 --> 00:10:58.497
That's how long I could last.

00:10:58.497 --> 00:10:59.801
I did his method, and we're doing

00:10:59.801 --> 00:11:01.797
this hyperventilation
stuff which looks like,

00:11:01.797 --> 00:11:04.840
(gasping and huffing)

00:11:04.840 --> 00:11:07.457
for like five or 10 or 15
minutes we're doing this.

00:11:07.457 --> 00:11:08.540
And then I hold my breath

00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:10.493
and I can hold my breath for two minutes,

00:11:10.493 --> 00:11:12.265
and I thought that was pretty cool.

00:11:12.265 --> 00:11:15.616
I didn't think it was a miracle,

00:11:15.616 --> 00:11:17.223
but I thought it was pretty cool.

00:11:17.223 --> 00:11:18.972
And then we kept on doing
that hyperventilation,

00:11:18.972 --> 00:11:20.454
and then he said,

00:11:20.454 --> 00:11:23.686
"Now, let all the air out of
your lungs and do pushups."

00:11:23.686 --> 00:11:25.453
And I knew I could do 20 pushups.

00:11:25.453 --> 00:11:28.506
I am not a extreme athlete or whatever,

00:11:28.506 --> 00:11:29.717
especially at that point.

00:11:29.717 --> 00:11:32.550
And I usually could do 20 pushups,

00:11:33.649 --> 00:11:36.055
but here I am, with no air
in my lungs doing pushups,

00:11:36.055 --> 00:11:38.763
and I put out 40 without breathing,

00:11:38.763 --> 00:11:41.238
and I thought that was just so cool.

00:11:41.238 --> 00:11:42.777
And that was probably the
moment where I'm like,

00:11:42.777 --> 00:11:44.512
"Okay, I'm going to give this guy a shot

00:11:44.512 --> 00:11:47.489
"to see what is actually happening."

00:11:47.489 --> 00:11:50.239
And then, like you said, he said,

00:11:51.749 --> 00:11:53.570
"Why don't we go stand out in the snow?"

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:56.320
And this is the winter of Poland,

00:11:58.474 --> 00:12:00.517
so it's the winter that
stopped the Nazi army.

00:12:00.517 --> 00:12:03.459
It's the winter that stopped Napoleon.

00:12:03.459 --> 00:12:05.780
And it's like January.

00:12:05.780 --> 00:12:08.424
And we stripped down
to nothing, basically.

00:12:08.424 --> 00:12:10.563
I had a bathing suit on, that's it.

00:12:10.563 --> 00:12:11.765
Stand in the snow.

00:12:11.765 --> 00:12:13.249
And it freaking hurts.

00:12:13.249 --> 00:12:16.775
Goddammit, I tell you,
it sucked 100 percent.

00:12:16.775 --> 00:12:19.947
My feet, it's like they're on coals.

00:12:19.947 --> 00:12:22.188
After five minutes, I run into a sauna,

00:12:22.188 --> 00:12:24.947
and it hurts even more
when I'm in the sauna.

00:12:24.947 --> 00:12:27.177
Warming up sucks even more.

00:12:27.177 --> 00:12:29.916
And then I was like,
"Wim, you're an asshole,

00:12:29.916 --> 00:12:32.199
"and I don't want to talk to you anymore,"

00:12:32.199 --> 00:12:34.622
but he's like, "Yeah, yeah, I get that.

00:12:34.622 --> 00:12:35.950
"That's always what happens

00:12:35.950 --> 00:12:37.221
"the first time you expose yourself.

00:12:37.221 --> 00:12:39.039
"Just see what happens tomorrow."

00:12:39.039 --> 00:12:41.723
And the next day, I go
out, and I land 10 minutes

00:12:41.723 --> 00:12:44.445
before that same feeling
of horror shows up.

00:12:44.445 --> 00:12:46.035
And the day after that, it's 30 minutes,

00:12:46.035 --> 00:12:47.340
and the day after that it's an hour,

00:12:47.340 --> 00:12:50.172
and I'm sweating at an hour.

00:12:50.172 --> 00:12:51.557
And I'm like, "Holy crap.

00:12:51.557 --> 00:12:52.724
"This is big."

00:12:56.579 --> 00:12:59.025
These are huge changes that
are happening really quickly,

00:12:59.025 --> 00:13:01.185
and I was just living in
Los Angeles before this,

00:13:01.185 --> 00:13:02.556
so that's like eternal summer.

00:13:02.556 --> 00:13:03.512
And going from there

00:13:03.512 --> 00:13:06.033
to this horrendously cold winter is crazy.

00:13:06.033 --> 00:13:08.513
And at the end of all
of the Polish retreats,

00:13:08.513 --> 00:13:10.671
you hike up this mountain called Snezka,

00:13:10.671 --> 00:13:15.213
which is a ski slope, but it
had just snowed the day before.

00:13:15.213 --> 00:13:18.624
And so we had like a foot of powder,

00:13:18.624 --> 00:13:20.723
that much powder, on the ground.

00:13:20.723 --> 00:13:24.198
And it took us eight
hours to get to the top,

00:13:24.198 --> 00:13:26.625
and I was just in nothing, basically,

00:13:26.625 --> 00:13:29.292
just shoes and shorts and a hat,

00:13:30.423 --> 00:13:32.303
and I was sweating the whole way up.

00:13:32.303 --> 00:13:35.479
And it just seemed so weird, right?

00:13:35.479 --> 00:13:37.013
Who knew that my body had this power?

00:13:37.013 --> 00:13:38.923
And it's not a miracle.

00:13:38.923 --> 00:13:42.699
It's not like he shot prana energy at me--

00:13:42.699 --> 00:13:45.185
(interviewers laughing)

00:13:45.185 --> 00:13:47.629
The thing that's really cool was that

00:13:47.629 --> 00:13:50.009
Wim's method is not a superpower.

00:13:50.009 --> 00:13:52.230
It's just an ordinary human power

00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:55.779
that we have left dormant
because we're all wimps

00:13:55.779 --> 00:13:57.804
and we like being in this comfortable

00:13:57.804 --> 00:14:02.698
72 degree temperature all the
time, having no variation,

00:14:02.698 --> 00:14:03.974
and so what Wim is doing is just

00:14:03.974 --> 00:14:07.251
putting variations into your daily routine

00:14:07.251 --> 00:14:09.225
and then triggering what is essentially

00:14:09.225 --> 00:14:11.521
evolutionary programming,
programming that we've had

00:14:11.521 --> 00:14:15.510
that has allowed our species
to survive and get us to now.

00:14:15.510 --> 00:14:17.864
He's just saying, "Here's how
to reactivate that stuff."

00:14:17.864 --> 00:14:20.478
(inspirational music)

00:14:20.478 --> 00:14:22.724
So Wim has done all of these crazy things.

00:14:22.724 --> 00:14:24.055
He's able to sit in the ice

00:14:24.055 --> 00:14:25.199
and control his body temperature,

00:14:25.199 --> 00:14:27.969
and they put a rectal probe in him to show

00:14:27.969 --> 00:14:29.457
that his body temperature rose in the ice

00:14:29.457 --> 00:14:30.940
instead of declined.

00:14:30.940 --> 00:14:33.120
And that was all very cool.

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:34.945
But then he made this claim

00:14:34.945 --> 00:14:39.437
that he could consciously
turn off, or turn up,

00:14:39.437 --> 00:14:41.542
his immune system at will,

00:14:41.542 --> 00:14:46.224
which is at the point thought
physiologically impossible.

00:14:46.224 --> 00:14:47.456
There's no mental connection

00:14:47.456 --> 00:14:50.789
between the immune system and the brain.

00:14:51.727 --> 00:14:52.814
But he said he could do it,

00:14:52.814 --> 00:14:55.527
and so these immunologists
over at Radboud University,

00:14:55.527 --> 00:14:57.667
in Nijmegen, there you go, got it,

00:14:57.667 --> 00:15:00.949
named Dr. Peter Pickkers and Matthijs Kox,

00:15:00.949 --> 00:15:05.509
what they did is devised a
test to see if he was right.

00:15:05.509 --> 00:15:08.009
And the test originally exists

00:15:09.492 --> 00:15:13.659
for testing cyclosporine and
other immunosuppressants,

00:15:16.928 --> 00:15:19.693
which are used if you
get a kidney transplant.

00:15:19.693 --> 00:15:22.186
Your body will usually reject the kidney

00:15:22.186 --> 00:15:24.448
and it'll eat up the kidney,

00:15:24.448 --> 00:15:27.715
and so what they need to do
is turn off your immune system

00:15:27.715 --> 00:15:30.297
in order that you can
keep the kidney around.

00:15:30.297 --> 00:15:34.033
So the way you test those drugs
is you give someone the drug

00:15:34.033 --> 00:15:37.429
and then inject them with this
heat killed E. coli bacteria,

00:15:37.429 --> 00:15:39.977
which is called endotoxin,

00:15:39.977 --> 00:15:43.667
which in 99 percent of normal people

00:15:43.667 --> 00:15:45.295
you get injected with it

00:15:45.295 --> 00:15:47.204
and you get a fever, a runny nose.

00:15:47.204 --> 00:15:50.871
You have all of the
primary immune responses

00:15:52.024 --> 00:15:54.509
until your body figures out
that it has been tricked

00:15:54.509 --> 00:15:56.803
and then it gives the big middle finger

00:15:56.803 --> 00:15:59.712
and then it reverts to normal.

00:15:59.712 --> 00:16:02.790
Now, Wim said that he could
do basically the same thing

00:16:02.790 --> 00:16:06.467
as taking an immunosuppressant,
but with his mind.

00:16:06.467 --> 00:16:08.887
- Which sounds super woo-woo.

00:16:08.887 --> 00:16:11.547
If you know nothing about
this, this sounds so woo-woo

00:16:11.547 --> 00:16:15.262
and saying you can control
your autonomic nervous system,

00:16:15.262 --> 00:16:18.415
any doctor is going to
say, "That's impossible."

00:16:18.415 --> 00:16:20.473
- The definition of autonomic means

00:16:20.473 --> 00:16:22.223
you don't freaking control it.

00:16:22.223 --> 00:16:24.667
It's automatic, right?

00:16:24.667 --> 00:16:27.681
And actually, a lot of
stuff on this journey

00:16:27.681 --> 00:16:29.159
ends up sounding woo-woo,

00:16:29.159 --> 00:16:31.909
and I am allergic to a lot of it,

00:16:32.915 --> 00:16:34.715
but you got to keep testing it,

00:16:34.715 --> 00:16:36.954
and some of this works out.

00:16:36.954 --> 00:16:38.958
So they inject him with endotoxin.

00:16:38.958 --> 00:16:40.290
He was supposed to get a fever,

00:16:40.290 --> 00:16:41.706
and he was like, "Oh, no, I feel fine.

00:16:41.706 --> 00:16:43.246
"I just have a minor headache."

00:16:43.246 --> 00:16:44.290
And that was all he said.

00:16:44.290 --> 00:16:46.279
And then they were doing blood tests

00:16:46.279 --> 00:16:47.771
and doing all these things.

00:16:47.771 --> 00:16:51.262
And his blood actually
remained resistant to endotoxin

00:16:51.262 --> 00:16:53.520
even after it was
extracted out of his body

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:56.899
and remained that way for
three days afterwards.

00:16:56.899 --> 00:16:58.735
So it was shocking.

00:16:58.735 --> 00:17:01.184
And there was this big
journal article that was like,

00:17:01.184 --> 00:17:03.697
"Look at this (audio cuts out)"

00:17:03.697 --> 00:17:07.410
And then some scientists saw this study,

00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:09.216
and they're like, "Well, wait a minute.

00:17:09.216 --> 00:17:10.980
"Wim is probably a genetic freak."

00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:14.063
Remember, I said 99 percent of people

00:17:16.409 --> 00:17:18.006
react in a predictable way,

00:17:18.006 --> 00:17:20.330
but one percent are able to resist

00:17:20.330 --> 00:17:22.692
or don't have that reaction.

00:17:22.692 --> 00:17:25.799
So that's a totally fair
scientific assessment.

00:17:25.799 --> 00:17:28.142
So what they did is a year later,

00:17:28.142 --> 00:17:31.206
Pickkers and Kox gathered 12,

00:17:31.206 --> 00:17:32.670
actually it was 18 volunteers,

00:17:32.670 --> 00:17:35.197
six people in a control
group, 12 in an active group,

00:17:35.197 --> 00:17:36.358
and they took those 12 guys

00:17:36.358 --> 00:17:38.559
and they put them through
Wim's Poland training,

00:17:38.559 --> 00:17:40.211
the same one that I did in Poland.

00:17:40.211 --> 00:17:42.673
Actually, they were there the week after

00:17:42.673 --> 00:17:47.418
I was reporting on the
Playboy story, same winter.

00:17:47.418 --> 00:17:50.689
After that, they brought
them all back to Radboud

00:17:50.689 --> 00:17:52.928
and injected them all with endotoxin,

00:17:52.928 --> 00:17:55.218
and they repeated the same results.

00:17:55.218 --> 00:17:57.227
Now, that journal article has been the one

00:17:57.227 --> 00:17:58.949
that everyone is now looking at,

00:17:58.949 --> 00:18:01.446
because what it proved is that

00:18:01.446 --> 00:18:03.274
not only was Wim really interesting,

00:18:03.274 --> 00:18:05.268
and look, he can suppress
his immune system,

00:18:05.268 --> 00:18:09.435
but that he can teach it
and that it's generalizable.

00:18:10.505 --> 00:18:13.992
And there's still ongoing
research, but that is the key,

00:18:13.992 --> 00:18:16.681
because if you can suppress
your immune system,

00:18:16.681 --> 00:18:19.746
then what does that mean
for autoimmune illnesses

00:18:19.746 --> 00:18:23.246
like arthritis or lupus or Crohn's disease

00:18:25.207 --> 00:18:29.518
where you have lesions from
your mouth to your butt,

00:18:29.518 --> 00:18:32.018
and what the implications are?

00:18:33.341 --> 00:18:34.973
And what I've seen personally

00:18:34.973 --> 00:18:37.036
from interviews and meeting people is that

00:18:37.036 --> 00:18:39.765
many folks are able to
reverse autoimmune illnesses

00:18:39.765 --> 00:18:42.216
by doing stuff like the Wim Hof method,

00:18:42.216 --> 00:18:45.058
this breathing, this conscious focus,

00:18:45.058 --> 00:18:48.490
this ice baths and that sort of stuff.

00:18:48.490 --> 00:18:49.719
And it's amazing.

00:18:49.719 --> 00:18:51.518
This has potential impacts

00:18:51.518 --> 00:18:55.287
for every autoimmune illness
out there in the world,

00:18:55.287 --> 00:18:57.305
anything where your body
is attacking itself,

00:18:57.305 --> 00:19:01.042
so think arthritis, think
lupus, think Crohn's disease.

00:19:01.042 --> 00:19:02.559
To some degree, diabetes.

00:19:02.559 --> 00:19:04.892
To some degree, Parkinson's.

00:19:07.650 --> 00:19:08.967
All of a sudden, all of these people

00:19:08.967 --> 00:19:13.134
are starting to use the Wim
Hof method to treat themselves

00:19:14.652 --> 00:19:18.492
for a huge variety of
autoimmune illnesses.

00:19:18.492 --> 00:19:20.552
And in the book, I look at

00:19:20.552 --> 00:19:23.995
four case studies of four different people

00:19:23.995 --> 00:19:27.990
who are using cold showers
and conscious breathing

00:19:27.990 --> 00:19:31.748
to combat and reverse, in some cases,

00:19:31.748 --> 00:19:35.415
Crohn's disease,
arthritis, and Parkinson's.

00:19:40.417 --> 00:19:41.891
They're amazing anecdotes.

00:19:41.891 --> 00:19:45.604
These are not totally randomized,
placebo controlled studies

00:19:45.604 --> 00:19:47.211
that have gone on for 15 years.

00:19:47.211 --> 00:19:49.764
But there are great bleeding edge results,

00:19:49.764 --> 00:19:52.219
and they're real people
and I've seen them myself.

00:19:52.219 --> 00:19:54.470
And I can say also in my own experience,

00:19:54.470 --> 00:19:55.965
having done this breathing method

00:19:55.965 --> 00:19:58.795
for about three, sorry, six years,

00:19:58.795 --> 00:20:01.932
since a kid, I have always
been generally healthy

00:20:01.932 --> 00:20:03.036
except for just one thing,

00:20:03.036 --> 00:20:05.021
which is I've always gotten canker sores,

00:20:05.021 --> 00:20:06.976
which are sort of like mouth ulcers.

00:20:06.976 --> 00:20:09.002
And because I was exposed to

00:20:09.002 --> 00:20:12.430
whatever the virus is that
starts it at a very young age,

00:20:12.430 --> 00:20:13.952
I started getting them at one,

00:20:13.952 --> 00:20:17.500
they could grow to be about
the size of a dime in my mouth,

00:20:17.500 --> 00:20:20.157
which means, for me, they're
just incredibly painful,

00:20:20.157 --> 00:20:23.760
difficult to talk, difficult to smile.

00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:26.266
And I had tried everything in the world

00:20:26.266 --> 00:20:27.691
to try to combat this.

00:20:27.691 --> 00:20:28.830
I tried alternative medicines.

00:20:28.830 --> 00:20:30.732
I tried Western medicines.

00:20:30.732 --> 00:20:32.762
I did stupid things
like putting salt on it

00:20:32.762 --> 00:20:33.795
'cause somebody told me that would work.

00:20:33.795 --> 00:20:34.975
It was horrible.

00:20:34.975 --> 00:20:35.933
- Painful.

00:20:35.933 --> 00:20:40.029
- Since doing this method, I
haven't gotten them at all.

00:20:40.029 --> 00:20:41.513
When I do the breathing,
when I do the cold,

00:20:41.513 --> 00:20:43.267
these things don't surface,

00:20:43.267 --> 00:20:45.111
and they used to surface
for me every two weeks.

00:20:45.111 --> 00:20:47.733
So for me, that's been amazing.

00:20:47.733 --> 00:20:50.467
Not quite as impressive as
Crohn's disease or arthritis,

00:20:50.467 --> 00:20:51.848
but still very cool.

00:20:51.848 --> 00:20:54.670
(inspirational music)

00:20:54.670 --> 00:20:57.752
There's two really easy methods

00:20:57.752 --> 00:21:00.250
to just start seeing
what your body can do,

00:21:00.250 --> 00:21:02.667
which is take a shower, wash.

00:21:04.180 --> 00:21:05.411
Easy so far, right?

00:21:05.411 --> 00:21:06.328
Super easy.

00:21:07.225 --> 00:21:08.697
Now, turn to the back of the shower

00:21:08.697 --> 00:21:10.682
and do the hardest thing
you'll ever do in your life

00:21:10.682 --> 00:21:13.994
and turn that knob to
cold, which really is hard.

00:21:13.994 --> 00:21:17.201
(Scott mumbles)
(interviewer laughs)

00:21:17.201 --> 00:21:18.953
This is really hard to
do, because you're like,

00:21:18.953 --> 00:21:20.208
"I was warm, though."

00:21:20.208 --> 00:21:22.971
And then sit in that cold, and then relax.

00:21:22.971 --> 00:21:23.888
Just chill.

00:21:25.012 --> 00:21:26.560
And it can be hard at first

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:28.766
because your response
could be very strong,

00:21:28.766 --> 00:21:31.999
but you can do it, I promise you.

00:21:31.999 --> 00:21:35.792
And relax there and just feel
that cold and then realize,

00:21:35.792 --> 00:21:38.754
I know you've probably swam
in the ocean at some point,

00:21:38.754 --> 00:21:40.279
and you realized when you
first got in it was cold

00:21:40.279 --> 00:21:41.764
because you tiptoed in,

00:21:41.764 --> 00:21:42.984
and then eventually you jumped in,

00:21:42.984 --> 00:21:44.188
and then you swam around
and it was all right.

00:21:44.188 --> 00:21:45.997
You're going to get to that same spot.

00:21:45.997 --> 00:21:46.997
And do that.

00:21:48.684 --> 00:21:49.517
Do that.

00:21:51.174 --> 00:21:53.794
That's the entry point that
you can do every morning.

00:21:53.794 --> 00:21:57.437
Do it first thing, and then
you'll get this great rush.

00:21:57.437 --> 00:21:58.799
When you get out, you'll be like,

00:21:58.799 --> 00:22:00.989
"Oh, everything is sort of easier today,

00:22:00.989 --> 00:22:02.550
"and I feel like a glow."

00:22:02.550 --> 00:22:04.293
That will totally happen.

00:22:04.293 --> 00:22:08.460
Another thing that I
recommend to people is going,

00:22:09.702 --> 00:22:12.869
if it's winter, going on a run outside

00:22:14.454 --> 00:22:16.085
with just your skivvies on,

00:22:16.085 --> 00:22:17.500
or if you're a woman, sports bra.

00:22:17.500 --> 00:22:20.154
- Yeah. (laughs)

00:22:20.154 --> 00:22:21.724
- And shoes.

00:22:21.724 --> 00:22:22.958
And go for a run,

00:22:22.958 --> 00:22:26.286
and just really just
not very much clothing,

00:22:26.286 --> 00:22:29.661
and just realize that
the first minute or two

00:22:29.661 --> 00:22:31.303
is going to be cold,
but then you're going to

00:22:31.303 --> 00:22:32.427
build up some internal heat.

00:22:32.427 --> 00:22:34.721
Or if you really want to be prepared,

00:22:34.721 --> 00:22:36.287
you can do some pushups before you go out

00:22:36.287 --> 00:22:38.741
so you've already built up some heat.

00:22:38.741 --> 00:22:40.578
Go for a mile or two in

00:22:40.578 --> 00:22:44.728
like 30 degree weather
or 20 degree weather

00:22:44.728 --> 00:22:45.968
in a place where,

00:22:45.968 --> 00:22:47.753
you can look at a frostbite
table if you want.

00:22:47.753 --> 00:22:49.913
You can just google, "Frostbite
table," and you can see.

00:22:49.913 --> 00:22:51.768
"Well, I can stay out for 30 minutes

00:22:51.768 --> 00:22:52.998
"before frostbite might be a danger."

00:22:52.998 --> 00:22:55.583
Okay, so do 29 minutes and
you're going to be all right.

00:22:55.583 --> 00:22:57.981
And you're going to generate
so much heat anyway,

00:22:57.981 --> 00:23:00.772
because when you run you generate
heat and you will be warm,

00:23:00.772 --> 00:23:03.256
that you're going to
realize this is great.

00:23:03.256 --> 00:23:05.040
And what's really cool,

00:23:05.040 --> 00:23:06.749
what's happening is that your nerves,

00:23:06.749 --> 00:23:08.298
even though you're
generating internal heat,

00:23:08.298 --> 00:23:09.542
the nerves under your skin

00:23:09.542 --> 00:23:12.145
are getting those messages saying,

00:23:12.145 --> 00:23:15.420
"It's winter, and winter is coming,

00:23:15.420 --> 00:23:17.470
"and therefore I'm going to
have to go get ready for this,"

00:23:17.470 --> 00:23:20.019
and your body starts
to adapt super quickly,

00:23:20.019 --> 00:23:22.782
and it's more or less painless to do.

00:23:22.782 --> 00:23:26.251
And you know, I climbed
up to Mount Kilimanjaro.

00:23:26.251 --> 00:23:29.271
I made it to Gilman's
Point, which is about 85,

00:23:29.271 --> 00:23:31.218
sorry, 18,500 feet.

00:23:31.218 --> 00:23:35.393
I was in a bathing suit
almost the whole way up.

00:23:35.393 --> 00:23:36.397
You can, too.

00:23:36.397 --> 00:23:38.982
It got down to negative 30 at times.

00:23:38.982 --> 00:23:42.439
So use that as a guidepost of
where you might be able to,

00:23:42.439 --> 00:23:43.966
I'm not saying Kilimanjaro
is what you need to do,

00:23:43.966 --> 00:23:44.799
but that's a good start.

00:23:44.799 --> 00:23:47.009
If you just do something like turn down

00:23:47.009 --> 00:23:50.467
the thermostat in your house in the winter

00:23:50.467 --> 00:23:54.634
to about 60 degrees, that will
give you some passive input,

00:23:57.667 --> 00:23:58.726
and also cold showers.

00:23:58.726 --> 00:23:59.953
This will do something.

00:23:59.953 --> 00:24:01.264
This will tell your body,

00:24:01.264 --> 00:24:04.248
you're giving signals to your
body to start doing things.

00:24:04.248 --> 00:24:07.224
Now, if you want to do,
learn the Wim Hof breathing,

00:24:07.224 --> 00:24:08.584
'cause it's really quite simple.

00:24:08.584 --> 00:24:11.730
I do about 15 minutes a day of it.

00:24:11.730 --> 00:24:13.048
You can do more.

00:24:13.048 --> 00:24:14.548
You don't need to.

00:24:16.424 --> 00:24:19.548
Read the book, and there's
this stuff to think about.

00:24:19.548 --> 00:24:23.200
And remember, these are guidelines.

00:24:23.200 --> 00:24:26.415
The headline here is that
the environment is important,

00:24:26.415 --> 00:24:27.681
and the environment you inhabit

00:24:27.681 --> 00:24:29.267
and your relation to it is important

00:24:29.267 --> 00:24:30.818
and you should not take it for granted.

00:24:30.818 --> 00:24:32.179
I think the breathing method

00:24:32.179 --> 00:24:36.044
is absolutely critical to the method,

00:24:36.044 --> 00:24:37.390
and I think it speeds it up.

00:24:37.390 --> 00:24:39.468
I think it helps a lot.

00:24:39.468 --> 00:24:43.417
And what it is it's this hyperventilation,

00:24:43.417 --> 00:24:47.334
so it's like 30 or 40
deep, controlled breaths,

00:24:49.409 --> 00:24:52.251
in which you're blowing
off CO2 out of your system

00:24:52.251 --> 00:24:56.418
and you're increasing your O2
saturation up to 100 percent.

00:24:58.246 --> 00:25:02.011
And then you hold your breath
with the air out of your lungs

00:25:02.011 --> 00:25:05.294
and you try to get to that
point where you need to gasp,

00:25:05.294 --> 00:25:07.461
where you feel like you're
like, "I got to breathe."

00:25:07.461 --> 00:25:09.967
And by doing that, what you're doing is

00:25:09.967 --> 00:25:13.953
interacting with an autonomic
system in your body,

00:25:13.953 --> 00:25:15.794
which is where the gasp reflex is,

00:25:15.794 --> 00:25:18.199
and you're telling your
body that you should have

00:25:18.199 --> 00:25:21.255
some control over this one system.

00:25:21.255 --> 00:25:24.046
And it's the same thing
that you do in a cold shower

00:25:24.046 --> 00:25:25.702
when you're telling
yourself not to shiver.

00:25:25.702 --> 00:25:29.251
It's the same skill of
becoming mentally strong

00:25:29.251 --> 00:25:31.720
over an autonomic reflex.

00:25:31.720 --> 00:25:33.011
And what this does,

00:25:33.011 --> 00:25:35.272
and these two methods
works on different systems,

00:25:35.272 --> 00:25:37.537
parasympathetic and sympathetic
systems in the body,

00:25:37.537 --> 00:25:40.037
but what that does is give you

00:25:42.126 --> 00:25:44.204
more resistance in the cold

00:25:44.204 --> 00:25:48.371
and has this autonomic
trick, this autonomic ability

00:25:50.269 --> 00:25:53.933
to influence your immune
system down the road,

00:25:53.933 --> 00:25:56.048
which is incredibly useful.

00:25:56.048 --> 00:25:58.699
So I wouldn't recommend,
if you're super lazy

00:25:58.699 --> 00:26:01.669
and all you're going to do is
the cold shower, then great.

00:26:01.669 --> 00:26:02.772
It's not going to be bad for you.

00:26:02.772 --> 00:26:03.810
That's awesome.

00:26:03.810 --> 00:26:04.768
That will help.

00:26:04.768 --> 00:26:07.670
But really, the breathing
method only adds 12 minutes

00:26:07.670 --> 00:26:08.977
to a morning workout.

00:26:08.977 --> 00:26:10.296
I wake up in the morning.

00:26:10.296 --> 00:26:11.809
I don't even get out of bed.

00:26:11.809 --> 00:26:14.981
I just lie there next to my
wife, who's also doing this,

00:26:14.981 --> 00:26:17.782
and we start with the breathing method,

00:26:17.782 --> 00:26:21.637
which is 30 deep,
controlled, rapid breaths

00:26:21.637 --> 00:26:22.952
that would look something like this.

00:26:22.952 --> 00:26:23.785
It would go ...

00:26:23.785 --> 00:26:26.949
(gasping and huffing)

00:26:26.949 --> 00:26:29.138
Like a little stop at the top and you go,

00:26:29.138 --> 00:26:30.230
and you do about 30 of those.

00:26:30.230 --> 00:26:32.426
You'll get lightheaded, maybe
you'll have tingly fingers,

00:26:32.426 --> 00:26:34.028
something like that.

00:26:34.028 --> 00:26:37.031
And then at the end of it,
you let all the air out,

00:26:37.031 --> 00:26:39.756
(exhales sharply) and hold it.

00:26:39.756 --> 00:26:42.206
And I usually will have
a stopwatch with me.

00:26:42.206 --> 00:26:43.733
On my iPhone, there's one.

00:26:43.733 --> 00:26:45.974
And then I hold it for
about a minute like that,

00:26:45.974 --> 00:26:47.310
and then I repeat.

00:26:47.310 --> 00:26:50.470
I do 30 breaths, and then air out,

00:26:50.470 --> 00:26:52.965
and I do a stopwatch and
I do it for two minutes,

00:26:52.965 --> 00:26:54.677
I hold my breathe for two
minutes, and the third time,

00:26:54.677 --> 00:26:58.344
I'll do the same rep but I'll
hold it for three minutes.

00:26:58.344 --> 00:27:00.028
And people will have different times.

00:27:00.028 --> 00:27:01.280
Don't worry too much about the times.

00:27:01.280 --> 00:27:04.181
Just be sure that you're
improving with every one,

00:27:04.181 --> 00:27:07.479
and that's usually not an issue.

00:27:07.479 --> 00:27:09.716
And then I'll do one
more rep of breathing,

00:27:09.716 --> 00:27:12.480
and I'll get out of my bed and
I'll do breath out pushups,

00:27:12.480 --> 00:27:14.022
so all the air out of my lungs

00:27:14.022 --> 00:27:16.948
and I just start doing pushups,
and I do 50 every morning.

00:27:16.948 --> 00:27:19.264
And I end up with, I
don't know why I do this,

00:27:19.264 --> 00:27:20.484
but I do a headstand at the end of it,

00:27:20.484 --> 00:27:21.832
and then I give my wife a hug,

00:27:21.832 --> 00:27:24.174
and then I'm ready to go eat breakfast.

00:27:24.174 --> 00:27:27.537
And I want to tell you that it
snowed in Denver last night,

00:27:27.537 --> 00:27:29.996
which means we got about a foot of snow,

00:27:29.996 --> 00:27:32.964
and according to the city, we
have to go shovel our walkway,

00:27:32.964 --> 00:27:35.163
so I wanted to give you
a quick winter workout

00:27:35.163 --> 00:27:38.203
that really anyone can
do, and it's a lot of fun.

00:27:38.203 --> 00:27:41.474
At the moment, it's about two
degrees Fahrenheit in Denver.

00:27:41.474 --> 00:27:42.666
Let's go.

00:27:42.666 --> 00:27:45.666
(rapid piano music)

00:28:17.626 --> 00:28:18.459
All right.

00:28:18.459 --> 00:28:19.322
That was a lot of fun.

00:28:19.322 --> 00:28:20.913
I hope you enjoy your winter.

00:28:20.913 --> 00:28:22.007
I know I will.

00:28:22.007 --> 00:28:24.504
And if you need a little bit more cold

00:28:24.504 --> 00:28:28.957
'cause you worked up a little
bit, there's always the snow.

00:28:28.957 --> 00:28:31.105
Pretty easy, and it feels great.

00:28:31.105 --> 00:28:31.938
Ah.

00:28:33.211 --> 00:28:35.211
(sighs)

00:28:44.108 --> 00:28:45.837
(laughs)

00:28:45.837 --> 00:28:47.593
(techno music)

00:28:47.593 --> 00:28:50.165
(inspirational music)

00:28:50.165 --> 00:28:51.738
- So thank you guys so much for watching.

00:28:51.738 --> 00:28:53.749
I'd love to know, what did
you think of this video?

00:28:53.749 --> 00:28:55.551
What do you think of the Wim Hof method?

00:28:55.551 --> 00:28:56.585
Have you tried it?

00:28:56.585 --> 00:28:59.491
I have definitely noticed
an improvement in my health

00:28:59.491 --> 00:29:02.205
since getting started, so I'm
extremely grateful for that.

00:29:02.205 --> 00:29:03.493
Let me know in the comments below.

00:29:03.493 --> 00:29:04.803
Also, if you want to check out

00:29:04.803 --> 00:29:07.682
the first Wim Hof video that
we made, go check that out.

00:29:07.682 --> 00:29:09.046
I think you'll learn a lot from it.

00:29:09.046 --> 00:29:11.993
And you could check out Scott's
book, What Doesn't Kill Us,

00:29:11.993 --> 00:29:13.757
wherever you pick up books.

00:29:13.757 --> 00:29:15.022
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:29:15.022 --> 00:29:16.027
I believe in you.

00:29:16.027 --> 00:29:17.977
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:29:17.977 --> 00:29:19.980
and whatever your one word is.

00:29:19.980 --> 00:29:21.247
Much love.

00:29:21.247 --> 00:29:22.465
I'll see you soon.

00:29:22.465 --> 00:29:23.686
(loud whoosh)

00:29:23.686 --> 00:29:25.154
(bang)

